//? Title: Symmetric QR: actual Householder steps and complete-row deflation
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A symmetric matrix encodes eigenvalues through coupled entries; reading its
diagonal before convergence can give the wrong spectrum. Shifted QR repeatedly
applies orthogonal similarities until a complete trailing row decouples. This
report reads actual native Householder iterations, retains the original
four-eigenvalue fixture and dense three-by-three convergence experiment, and
separates convergence from an iteration cap.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

For the active block, factor \(A-\mu I=QR\), with \(Q^TQ=I\), then form
$$ A^+=RQ+\mu I=Q^TAQ. $$
\alttext(The reversed shifted QR product is orthogonally similar to the current matrix.)
Similarity preserves the characteristic polynomial. Householder reflectors
remain orthogonal even when a shifted column vanishes; a zero column is skipped
rather than replaced by a zero orthogonal basis vector. The trailing two-by-two
block supplies a Wilkinson shift, computed with hypot and a
cancellation-avoiding formula.

A dense matrix can have \(A_{m-1,m-2}=0\) while other entries in its last row
are large. Consequently the native solver tests the complete trailing
off-diagonal norm against a diagonal scale, then zeros both symmetric couplings
only on acceptance. An exhausted per-block budget throws. This implementation
performs dense cubic-cost steps without a tridiagonal reduction, so the familiar
cubic local convergence claim for a specialized tridiagonal QR solver is not
asserted here.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/qr_algorithm_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/modular_exponentiation.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/integer_arithmetic.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <climits>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/number_theory.hpp>
#include <numeric>
#include <numbers>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <array>
#include <tuple>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/prufer.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/flow_diagram.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/hull_algorithms.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/sampling_analysis.hpp>
#include <iomanip>
#include <bit>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/qr_algorithm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    auto t = a::qr_eigenvalues_trace({{2, 1}, {1, 2}});
    std::print("\n| active order | actual step | actual shift | trailing norm | deflated |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (const auto &s : t.steps)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s.active, s.iteration,
                   s.shift, s.trailing_norm, s.deflated);

    g::Diagram d;
    d.node("n0", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual first active order\n{}", t.steps[0].active));
    d.node("n1", {10, 0},
           std::format("Actual first shift\n{}", t.steps[0].shift));
    d.node("n2", {20, 0},
           std::format("Actual final spectrum\n{}", t.eigenvalues));
    d.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    d.save_svg("build/qr_algorithm_tiny.svg");
}
